@meta
id: 18-08
title: Find by any part of the name
chapter: 18
type: lesson
skills: find-path, wildmenu
par: 41
minutes: 5
vscode: Ctrl+P "repo"
vimrc: set path=.,** wildmenu wildmode=longest:full,full
open: README.md

@brief
The soft-delete column was renamed from `deleted` to `deletedAt` in the
last migration. The order repository still filters on `deleted: null`
in `findByCustomer`. Fix that one key. The customer repository has no
such filter and needs nothing.

You remember "repository", not what comes before it. A prefix will not
get you there. Put a `*` in front of the fragment and `:find *repo` Tab
matches any file with `repo` anywhere in its name. There are two, so
the first Tab fills in nothing and shows both; each further Tab moves to
the next candidate. Enter takes the one shown.

@keys
| `:find *frag` `Tab` | any file with `frag` anywhere in its name |
| `Tab` `Tab` | walk the candidates |
| `Shift-Tab` | walk backwards |
| `:find *.spec` `Tab` | works with any glob |

@hints
`:find *repo` and Tab. Two names appear in the bar. Tab again moves the
highlight.
--
The customer repository comes first because `c` sorts before `o`. One
more Tab reaches the order repository. Or type `*r.repo` — narrowing
with letters beats cycling when you can.
--
`:find *repo<Tab><Tab><Tab>` Enter, `/deleted` Enter, `ea`, `At`,
`Esc`, `:wq`.

@solution
    :find *repo<Tab><Tab><Tab><Enter> /deleted<Enter> eaAt <Esc> :wq<Enter>

or, narrowing instead of cycling:

    :find order.repo<Tab><Enter> ...

The glob is what makes `:find` behave like a fuzzy finder rather than an
autocomplete. `*` matches anything, so `*repo` is "contains repo",
`*.spec` is "ends in .spec…", and `order*` is the plain prefix you had
before. With `**` in `path`, all of it searches the whole tree.

When the bar shows more than two or three candidates, do not cycle.
Type another letter. Cycling is for when you genuinely cannot tell
which one you want until you see the list.

@start README.md
# Orders service

Handles orders, customers and refunds for the storefront.

Soft deletes: every table has a nullable `deletedAt` timestamp. Live
rows are the ones where it is null.

@start src/domain/order/order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public async findByCustomer(args: { customerId: string }) {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({
      where: { customerId: args.customerId, deleted: null },
    });

    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));
  }

  public toEntity(record: OrderRecord): Order {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      customerId: record.customerId,
      status: record.status,
      total: record.total,
    };
  }
}

@expected src/domain/order/order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public async findByCustomer(args: { customerId: string }) {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({
      where: { customerId: args.customerId, deletedAt: null },
    });

    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));
  }

  public toEntity(record: OrderRecord): Order {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      customerId: record.customerId,
      status: record.status,
      total: record.total,
    };
  }
}

@start src/domain/customer/customer.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Customer as CustomerRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Customer } from './customer.entity';

@injectable()
export class CustomerRepository extends BaseRepository<CustomerRecord, Customer> {
  public toEntity(record: CustomerRecord): Customer {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      email: record.email,
      name: record.name,
    };
  }
}

@start src/domain/order/order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';
